{"builder":"Goal: when a config file holds malformed JSON, the app's `bad config:` error must name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. `loadConfig` must keep throwing (do NOT catch the error and return `{}` or defaults), and valid configs must load exactly as before.\n\nCurrent code:\n- `src/config.js`: `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`.\n- `src/app.js`: calls `loadConfig(process.argv[2] ?? 'config.json')`, on error writes `bad config: ${error.message}\\n` to stderr and exits 1; otherwise prints `listening on <port>`.\n- `test/config.test.js`: tests run with `npm test` (`node --test`). One existing test asserts `loadConfig` on `{port: 9000` throws a `SyntaxError`; keep it passing (or update it consistently) — throwing a SyntaxError still is the simplest option.\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, read the file text, then wrap only the `JSON.parse` in try/catch. On a parse error, throw a new error (a SyntaxError is fine; set `cause` to the original) whose `message` includes the path exactly as passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path), plus the line and column of the mistake, e.g. `<path>:3:3: Expected double-quoted property name in JSON at position 20 (line 3 column 3)` or `<path>: line 3 column 3: ...`.\n2. Compute line and column yourself rather than relying on Node's message, because other Node versions may only give a position or nothing: if the original message has `(line N column M)` use that; otherwise if it has `position P`, count newlines in the text before offset P to get a 1-based line, and the column as P minus the index after the last newline before P, plus 1. If neither is available, still include the path. For the sample file below on Node v22 the result must be line 3, column 3 (position 20 → line 3, column 3).\n3. Leave read errors (e.g. ENOENT for a missing file) as they are; they must still lead to `bad config:` and exit 1 via `src/app.js`. Do not add schema validation or JSON5/comment support.\n4. `src/app.js` should need no change, since it prints `error.message`; make sure nothing is written to stdout on failure.\n5. Add tests in `test/config.test.js` that fail on the current code: (a) `loadConfig` on a temp file containing `{port: 9000` throws and the error `message` contains the temp file path; (b) for a file with the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}` the message contains the path and line 3 / column 3. Optionally add a test that spawns `node src/app.js <file>` and checks exit 1, empty stdout and stderr content. Keep the existing `reads a config` test (`{\"port\": 9000}` → `{ port: 9000 }`).\n6. Optionally update `README.md` to say the `bad config:` message names the file and location.\n7. Check by hand before opening the PR:\n   - `d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json; echo \"exit $?\"` → stderr has `bad config:`, `$d/c.json`, line 3 and column 3; exit 1; no stdout.\n   - `printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json` → `listening on 9000`, exit 0.\n   - `npm test` passes.\n8. Open one pull request with these changes, referencing https://github.com/o/settings/issues/9.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head from the repository root. Each command block is self-contained; shell variables do not carry between commands. Use the Node version available (v22).\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \\\"port\\\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \\\"x\\\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > $d/c.json; node src/app.js $d/c.json >$d/out 2>$d/err; echo \"exit $?\"; echo \"--stdout--\"; cat $d/out; echo \"--stderr--\"; cat $d/err; echo \"--path--\"; echo $d/c.json`\nPASS: prints `exit 1` and the stdout section is empty. FAIL: any other exit code, or anything on stdout (including a `listening on` line).\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nRun the same command as Check 1 and read the stderr section against the printed path.\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, contains the exact string printed under `--path--` (the `<tmp>/c.json` path as passed), and identifies the mistake's location as line 3 and column 3 (in any readable form, e.g. `line 3 column 3`, `3:3`, `line 3, col 3`). FAIL: any of these is missing, the path is altered (e.g. only the basename), or the line/column shown is not 3 and 3.\n\nCheck 3 — expect line: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > $d/b.json; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"`\nPASS: prints `OK`. FAIL: prints `RETURNED ...` or `NO PATH: ...`, or the import fails.\n\nCheck 4 — expect line: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun:\n`d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > $d/ok.json; node src/app.js $d/ok.json; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, { port: 9000 }); console.log('RETURN OK', JSON.stringify(v))\"`\nPASS: stdout shows `listening on 9000`, then `exit 0`, then `RETURN OK {\"port\":9000}`. FAIL: a different stdout, a nonzero exit, or the deepEqual assertion fails.\n\nCheck 5 — expect line: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nStep A: run `npm test` at the PR head. Step B: find the base commit with `git merge-base HEAD origin/HEAD` (if `origin/HEAD` is not set, use the PR's base commit from `gh pr view --json baseRefOid`). Then in a scratch copy, put the base commit's `src/` under the PR head's `test/`: `t=$(mktemp -d); git worktree add --detach $t HEAD; cd $t; git checkout <base-sha> -- src; npm test; echo \"exit $?\"` (then `git worktree remove --force $t`). Read the PR's test files to see what the failing tests assert.\nPASS: Step A passes, and in Step B at least one test fails whose assertion checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path. FAIL: `npm test` fails at the head, or no test asserting the path in the malformed-JSON error fails against the base `src/`.\n\nAdvisory (not part of PASS/FAIL):\n- For Check 2, read `src/config.js` / `src/app.js` to see whether the line/column comes only from Node's own message or is computed from the position too; note it if it relies solely on Node v22's message wording.\n- Note whether `loadConfig` swallows errors or returns defaults in any path (a non-goal), whether a missing config file still gives `bad config:` and exit 1, and whether the existing `bad JSON throws` SyntaxError test still exists.\n- Note whether the README was updated (optional)."}